 * | * | * | * |\n| F | . | . | @ | * | * | * | . | . |\n| G | . | . | * | * | . | . | . | . |\n| H | . | . | . | . | . | . | . | . |\nYour valid actions are(A, 4),(B, 6),(B, 7),(C, 8),(F, 7),(F, 8),(G, 5),(G, 6),(G, 7),(H, 3),(H, 5)\n", 'reasoning': '**Strategic Analysis and Move Selection: F8**\n\nOkay, I\'m staring at this Reversi board, playing as \'@\'. The opponent is \'*\'. Got it. Let\'s dig in. First things first, corners. A1 is mine, solid. A8 is empty, as are H1 and H8. Noted. Edge stability is critical. Column A is a mess, a single "@", then the opponent. Column H is totally empty. Row A is a strong presence by me, but Row H is empty. And Rows B, C, D have a bit of a mixed presence. Okay, coordinate format check, just to be sure - it\'s (Row Letter, Column Number).\n\nNow, the valid moves. A4 looks okay, extending my A1 corner\'s influence. But, A5 is next to a star, so I need to be careful not to make it easy for my opponent to get an upper hand. B6? Hmm, C-square. Not ideal, plus it flips a ton of pieces. B7 is a diagonal, also a C-square and again, many flips involved. Dangerous. C8 looks intriguing, connecting to my D8. However, not as promising as another move. F7 just seems okay. Now, let\'s zero in on F8.\n\nF8 is on the edge, the right-hand edge, to be precise. And it is adjacent to the opponent\'s piece at E8. I realize F8 might sandwich my opponent at E8 and turn it into my own, and it is right next to my D8 piece. This is promising. A solid line of "@" pieces along the edge. It\'s an edge piece. Okay, this is a strong block. My opponent will be denied and I will gain an advantage. Let\'s check for any immediate counterplay the opponent might have. Can they take any adjacent pieces in the next turn? Okay, and it has strategic importance on the right edge. Is G8 open? Does it give the opponent easy access to G8? No. The opponent doesn\'t have an immediate access there so far. The right side is secured.\n\nThe goal is to expand, control territory and secure edges. This F8 move immediately achieves it. Edge Control! The best move is now (F, 8). Time to place my piece!\n\n\n', 'output': "Situation: I have a strong anchor in the top-left corner (A1) and an isolated edge piece at D8. The opponent holds the adjacent edge piece at E8 and controls the center. My goal is to consolidate my edge presence and restrict the opponent's mobility.\n\nEvaluation: The most promising moves are (A, 4) and (F, 8).\n(A, 4) is a quiet move that extends my stable territory from the top-left corner along the A-row, flipping A5. It is a very safe move.\n(F, 8) is an edge move that uses my piece at D8 to flank and capture the opponent's edge piece at E8. It also flips internal diagonal pieces. This transforms the right edge from a contested state into a solid block of three friendly pieces (D8, E8, F8).\n\nReason: I choose (F, 8) because capturing the opponent's edge piece (E8) significantly shifts the structural advantage in my favor. It creates a connected group of three stable pieces on the right edge and removes the opponent's only foothold there. This is strategically more valuable than the safe extension at (A, 4).\n\nAnswer: (F, 8)"}
