 7):** An X-square. I avoid these unless there\'s a killer combo.\n\n8.  **(G, 2):** Another C-square! No thanks!\n\n9.  **(G, 5):** It\'s a central expansion; not terrible.\n\n10. **(G, 6):** Expanding again.\n\n11. **(G, 7):** Another X-Square.\n\n12. **(H, 2):** Edge piece, might open up a vulnerability.\n\n13. **(H, 3):** Might weaken a bottom edge.\n\n14. **(H, 5):** Empty edge move. Usually bad!\n\nOkay, so I have a shortlist. I\'m looking for a move that strengthens my position without being too risky. (A, 4) seems like the most professional. (G, 5) is tempting for the center, but (A, 4) is better.\n\nI\'m thinking (A, 4) strengthens the A-wall. Yes! It stabilizes. This isn\'t just about flipping pieces; it\'s about positioning. Playing (A, 4) it is, it strengthens the A-wall from the initial state, from three pieces in a row to four in a row, minimizing the opponent\'s chances to counter-attack. The best move is often the simple, stable one. (A, 4) is that move.\n\nDouble-checking: (A, 4). Right now, the board will show three @ in a row on A. (A, 4) adds a fourth. This will make my @ column stable. The diagonal looks safe too. I\'m confident.\n\nSo, the action is (A, 4). Time to play!\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the mid-game phase. I (Player \'@\') control the top-left corner (A1) and the upper portion of the left edge (A-row). The opponent (\'*\') has a strong presence in the center. My pieces on the A-row (A1, A2, A3) form a stable base. The opponent has a piece at A5, creating a gap at A4.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Move (A, 4): This is a "quiet" edge move. It places a piece at A4, connecting with my existing wall A1-A3. It captures diagonally (A4 -> B3 -> C2 -> D1), flipping only 2 pieces. It does not allow the opponent to immediately counter-attack on the A-row because A5 is already theirs, and they cannot sandwich A4 from A6 easily.\n- Move (B, 6): This move flips four pieces on row B. While it gains material, it creates a large block of pieces that might become targets for the opponent and potentially gives up mobility by flipping too many discs.\n- Moves like (H, 3) or (H, 5): These are moves on an empty edge (H-row). Taking an empty edge without corner support is generally risky as it can give the opponent a tempo or a target to wedge in.\n- Moves like (C, 8) or (B, 8): These place pieces on C-squares near empty corners, which is strategically dangerous as it often invites the opponent to take the corner.\n\nReason: (A, 4) is the best choice because it is a low-risk, positional move. It extends my control of the A-edge to A1-A4, consolidating a stable wall. By flipping only a few pieces, I preserve mobility for future turns. It does not open up any corners or weak points for the opponent to exploit.\n\nAnswer: (A, 4)'}
