eral corners (A8, H1, H8) are still empty. Key traps exist involving X-squares (B7, G2, G7) and C-squares (B8, G1, etc.) which must be avoided to prevent giving the opponent free corners.\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **(A, 6)**: This is a very strong move. It places a piece on the top edge at Row A, Column 6. It flips only A5 (* -> @), consolidating the entire A1-A6 strip as my territory. It is a "quiet" move (flips few pieces) and does not give the opponent access to the A8 corner because they cannot play A7 or A8 immediately after.\n2.  **(B, 7)**: This is an X-square move adjacent to the empty corner A8. Analysis shows that playing here allows the opponent to capture the A8 corner immediately by sandwiching the new piece. This is a losing move.\n3.  **(G, 7)**: Similarly, this X-square move gives the opponent the H8 corner.\n4.  **(F, 1)**: A reasonable edge extension on the left, but it flips more internal pieces (E2, D3, C4) than (A, 6) and is less structurally secure than completing the A-row.\n5.  **(G, 5)**: This move flips many pieces in the center. In Reversi strategy, flipping too many pieces mid-game often gives the opponent increased mobility, so this is less desirable than the stable (A, 6).\n\nReason: I choose (A, 6) because it maximizes stability by securing the top edge and minimizes the opponent\'s tactical options. It connects my existing corner group at A1 to A6, creating a solid wall that is difficult for the opponent to flank. It avoids the dangerous squares adjacent to empty corners and flips only a single piece, which is excellent for preserving mobility.\n\nAnswer: (A, 6)'}
