nal. This is a very safe and structurally sound move.\n- **(B, 8)**: This move captures the C-square on the right edge and flips the opponent's piece at C8. While it connects my pieces on the right edge (D8-F8), playing to a C-square adjacent to an empty corner (A8) is inherently risky as it may eventually allow the opponent to access the corner.\n- **(G, 6)**: This move flips a large number of pieces (entire column 6 and a diagonal). In Reversi, flipping too many pieces in the mid-game is often disadvantageous because it provides the opponent with many new valid move options (mobility).\n- **(H, 3)**: This is an edge move on the bottom row. However, it is isolated and not connected to a corner, leaving gaps (H1, H2) that the opponent might exploit later.\n\nReason: Move (F, 1) is the best choice because it extends my existing stable wall from the A1 corner down the left edge. Securing edges connected to corners is a fundamental strategy to limit the opponent's options and secure permanent territory. It invalidates the potential internal move at F2 (which would block F1) and flips internal pieces without giving the opponent easy counter-attacks on the edge.\n\nAnswer: (F, 1)"}
