e.\n\n*Action:* (G, 5)\n\n\n", 'output': "Situation: The game is in the mid-game phase. I currently hold a strong position on the left side and top-left corner, while the opponent controls the right side and the lower-right center. I have several candidate moves, but some are structurally dangerous. The key is to expand influence without giving up corners (A8, H1, H8).\n\nEvaluation: \n- Move (B, 7) is a move to a C-square (adjacent to corner A8). It flips pieces leading towards the corner, creating a bridge (E4-D5-C6-B7) that would allow the opponent to take corner A8 immediately. This is a bad move.\n- Move (G, 2) is also a C-square move (adjacent to corner H1). It flips G3, and the resulting configuration allows the opponent to sandwich my pieces from H1 (using E4 as an anchor), conceding corner H1. This is also a bad move.\n- Move (G, 7) is an X-square move (diagonally adjacent to the empty corner H8). Playing here is generally strategically poor as it often gives the corner to the opponent eventually.\n- Move (G, 6) captures a large number of pieces (Column 6) but places a piece on a C-square and invites the opponent to play G7, which could be destabilizing.\n- Move (H, 3) is a safe, quiet edge move, but it has low impact on the board state.\n- Move (G, 5) is a central move that flips 5 opponent pieces in multiple directions (Vertical: F5, E5; Diagonal: F4, E3, D2). It breaks up the opponent's central mass, increases my mobility, and does not immediately offer any corners to the opponent.\n\nReason: (G, 5) is the best strategic choice. It maximizes the number of flipped pieces and strengthens central control, forcing the opponent to play on the periphery. Crucially, unlike (B, 7) or (G, 2), it does not create an immediate tactical weakness that loses a corner. It is a proactive move that improves my position significantly compared to the passive (H, 3).\n\nAnswer: (G, 5)"}
